analysis:
This batch contains documentation only. The new changelog (changelog.d/qwen-end-of-prompt-checkpoints.md:1-13) and the new inference.md row (head line 1387, plus the date at line 3) describe a dense Qwen3.5/3.8 solo-prefill change. It ends an extra prefill range, and so a complete SSD checkpoint, just below the prompt end: at least 64 tokens before it, on the 256-token grid, when that saves at least the 1,024-token floor. The code is EngineV2Factory+DemandedCheckpoint.swift (demandedLatestCheckpointMinimumTokens).

Relative to T-041 and SEC-035, this adds one more at-rest checkpoint per qualifying request, deeper into the prompt (closer to the full conversation). The documented posture is unchanged: HMAC-keyed names, a coordinator-authored cacheSalt, a 30-minute TTL, AES-GCM with an SE-rooted KEK, and the 1,024-token benefit floor. The checkpoint stays at least 64 tokens short of the prompt end, so the final user tokens are not persisted. This is the same kind of exposure as existing stripe-end checkpoints; it is not new disclosure.

The source implementation is not in this batch. Integration should verify in EngineV2Factory+DemandedCheckpoint.swift and the CBv2 scheduler that:
- the demanded checkpoint still goes through the per-donation adoption/benefit gate, the repeat-hint gate and the cacheSalt scoping;
- it does not bypass the DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE kill switch;
- it is limited to Qwen35Model and not MoE models;
- extra checkpoint writes do not breach the staging reservation or endurance and disk guards (availability, T-016).

The TTFT timing-oracle granularity is somewhat finer, but it remains within the same-account scope accepted under SEC-035. No regression is evidenced by the docs alone.
